##" Every fatal US road crash 2019-2024 (NHTSA FARS): who died, where, when, alcohol (NHTSA's imputed blood alcohol), speeding, belts, road type, with vehicle-miles (FHWA) and population for rates, and deaths valued at US DOT's $14.2M value of a statistical life. 2019-2023 Final Files; 2024 is the Annual Report File (not final). Totals match NHTSA's published counts.
## dataset { title="US Fatal Road Crashes (FARS 2019 - 2024)" }

// Loaded by load_fars.sql into MotherDuck ltools.fars (222,695 crashes, 544,030 persons, 343,261 vehicles, 2019-2024).
// Sources: NHTSA FARS national CSVs (downloaded 2026-10-20); FHWA Highway Statistics VM-2 2019-2024; Census county
// population estimates (via ltools.libraries.county_population). VSL: US DOT departmental guidance, $14.2M for base
// year 2025 (page effective 2026-03-20).

#" One row per fatal crash, 2019-2024: deaths, place, road type, light, time, and crash-level flags for alcohol, speeding and hit-and-run.
#(agent) Count deaths with `deaths` (sum of fatalities), never with a count of crashes (7.5% low) or of people involved. ST_CASE repeats every year; `crash_key` includes the year.
#(agent) Alcohol: use `alcohol_impaired_deaths` (NHTSA's method: average over 10 imputed blood-alcohol values of the highest driver BAC, .08+). Tested results only (`alcohol_deaths_tested_only`) are 45% low in 2024 and fall when fewer results are reported, so they make false trends (NC 2021-2023: -53% tested-only, -12% imputed).
#(agent) Speeding is a crash flag (any driver speeding); count `speeding_related_deaths`, not deaths per speeding vehicle (23% low). Night = 6pm-5:59am; weekend = Fri 6pm-Mon 5:59am (NHTSA definitions).
#(agent) `year` 2024 is NHTSA's Annual Report File (`file_status`): its counts will be revised, and 2024 BAC results are more often missing. Road type labels changed in 2023 in the raw files; `road_class`/`road_function` here are built from codes and are consistent. Unknown codes are null or 'Unknown'.
#(agent) Values: every `*_valued_at_vsl` measure = deaths x $14.2M (US DOT VSL, 2025 dollars; DOT's sensitivity range is $8.52M-$19.88M, the `_low`/`_high` measures). They SUM OVER EVERY YEAR IN THE QUERY: for a yearly value group by `year` or divide by the number of years, and say which. Values of overlapping groups (alcohol, arterials, pedestrians) must not be added together.
#(agent) For a per-mile or per-resident rate use the `road_exposure` or `state_years` sources; crashes has no denominator. There is no county mileage: county rates are per resident only.
#(agent) North Carolina's FARS speeding-related count rose from 2022 to 2024 while deaths in crashes with a vehicle recorded 10+ mph over the limit fell: see `nc_speeding_definitions` before quoting an NC speeding trend.
source: crashes is motherduck.table('fars.crashes') extend {
  primary_key: crash_key

  dimension:
    #" 'North Carolina' or 'Rest of US', for the worked-example comparisons.
    nc_or_rest_of_us is state_name ? pick 'North Carolina' when 'North Carolina' else 'Rest of US'
    #" 'Wake County', 'Rest of NC' or 'Outside NC'.
    wake_or_rest_of_nc is pick 'Wake County' when county_fips = '37183' pick 'Rest of NC' when state_name = 'North Carolina' else 'Outside NC'

  measure:
    #" Fatal crashes (not deaths).
    fatal_crashes is count()
    #" People killed within 30 days of the crash.
    deaths is fatalities.sum()
    #" Deaths valued at US DOT's value of a statistical life, $14.2 million (2025 dollars) per death; sums over the years in the query.
    # currency
    deaths_valued_at_vsl is fatalities.sum() * 14200000
    #" Alcohol-impaired-driving deaths, NHTSA's definition and method: deaths in crashes where a driver had BAC .08+, averaged over NHTSA's 10 imputations (reproduces NHTSA's published counts).
    alcohol_impaired_deaths is sum(fatalities * ai_share)
    #" Share of deaths that were alcohol-impaired-driving deaths (imputed, NHTSA method).
    # percent
    alcohol_impaired_share is sum(fatalities * ai_share) / nullif(fatalities.sum(), 0)
    #" Alcohol-impaired-driving deaths valued at $14.2M each (2025 dollars); sums over the years in the query.
    # currency
    alcohol_impaired_deaths_valued_at_vsl is sum(fatalities * ai_share) * 14200000
    #" For comparison only, undercounts: deaths in crashes where a driver's TESTED BAC was .08+ (untested drivers count as sober).
    alcohol_deaths_tested_only is fatalities.sum() { where: tested_driver_bac_08 }
    #" For comparison only: deaths in crashes where police reported a drinking driver.
    alcohol_deaths_police_reported is fatalities.sum() { where: police_reported_drinking_driver }
    #" Deaths in speeding-related crashes (any driver speeding, racing or too fast for conditions; NHTSA definition).
    speeding_related_deaths is fatalities.sum() { where: speeding_related }
    #" Deaths in crashes where a vehicle's recorded travel speed was 10+ mph over the posted limit (travel speed is unknown for most vehicles outside a few states, incl. NC where it is known for ~95%).
    deaths_vehicle_10_over_limit is fatalities.sum() { where: vehicle_10_over_limit }
    #" Share of vehicles in these crashes with both travel speed and posted limit recorded.
    # percent
    speed_known_share is vehicles_speed_known.sum() / nullif(vehicles_in_transport.sum(), 0)
    #" Deaths valued at the low end of DOT's VSL range ($8.52M, 2025 dollars); sums over the years in the query.
    # currency
    deaths_valued_at_vsl_low is fatalities.sum() * 8520000
    #" Deaths valued at the high end of DOT's VSL range ($19.88M, 2025 dollars); sums over the years in the query.
    # currency
    deaths_valued_at_vsl_high is fatalities.sum() * 19880000
    #" Share of alcohol-impaired-driving deaths (imputed) NOT backed by a crash with a tested driver at .08+: how much of the count rests on NHTSA's imputation.
    # percent
    alcohol_share_resting_on_imputation is 1 - fatalities.sum() { where: tested_driver_bac_08 } / nullif(sum(fatalities * ai_share), 0)
    #" Deaths in hit-and-run crashes.
    hit_and_run_deaths is fatalities.sum() { where: hit_and_run }

  #" F2. Alcohol-impaired-driving deaths by year, counted three ways: NHTSA's imputed method (the right one), tested results only, and police-reported drinking. Add `where: state_name = 'North Carolina'` for a state.
  view: alcohol_counting_methods is {
    group_by: `year`, file_status
    aggregate: deaths, alcohol_impaired_deaths, alcohol_impaired_share, alcohol_deaths_tested_only, alcohol_deaths_police_reported, alcohol_impaired_deaths_valued_at_vsl
    order_by: `year`
  }

  #" F2. North Carolina vs rest of US by year: alcohol-impaired-driving deaths (imputed), deaths backed by a tested .08+ driver, and the share resting on imputation.
  view: nc_vs_rest_alcohol_testing is {
    group_by: `year`, nc_or_rest_of_us
    aggregate: alcohol_impaired_deaths, alcohol_deaths_tested_only, alcohol_share_resting_on_imputation
    order_by: `year`, nc_or_rest_of_us
  }

  #" Deaths by year with NHTSA's headline categories (alcohol imputed, speeding, night, rural/urban), for checking against NHTSA's publications.
  view: deaths_by_year is {
    group_by: `year`, file_status
    aggregate:
      deaths, fatal_crashes, alcohol_impaired_deaths, speeding_related_deaths, deaths_valued_at_vsl
      night_deaths is fatalities.sum() { where: day_night = 'Nighttime (6pm-5:59am)' }
      rural_deaths is fatalities.sum() { where: rural_urban = 'Rural' }
      urban_deaths is fatalities.sum() { where: rural_urban = 'Urban' }
    order_by: `year`
  }
}

#" One row per motor vehicle in transport in a fatal crash: vehicle type (from body type), speeding, travel speed, posted limit, drinking driver.
#(agent) `occupant_deaths` counts only this vehicle's occupants (no pedestrians); for deaths use `crashes.deaths` or `persons.deaths`. `travel_speed_mph` is unknown for about 60% of vehicles.
#(agent) `vehicle_type` comes from FARS body type; NHTSA's reports since 2020 use VIN decoding, so car vs SUV counts differ from NHTSA's by about 1,000 a year. Passenger-vehicle, motorcycle and large-truck totals agree within 0.2%.
source: vehicles is motherduck.table('fars.vehicles') extend {
  primary_key: vehicle_key
  join_one: crashes on crash_key = crashes.crash_key

  dimension:
    #" True when recorded travel speed is 10+ mph over the posted limit (null when either is unknown).
    over_limit_by_10 is travel_speed_mph >= posted_speed_limit_mph + 10

  measure:
    #" Vehicles in fatal crashes.
    vehicle_count is count()
    #" Vehicles with recorded travel speed 10+ mph over the posted limit.
    vehicles_10_over is count() { where: over_limit_by_10 }
    #" Of the vehicles recorded 10+ mph over the limit, the share FARS flags as speeding-related.
    # percent
    flagged_share_of_10_over is count() { where: over_limit_by_10 and speeding_related } / nullif(count() { where: over_limit_by_10 }, 0)
    #" Of the vehicles with recorded travel speed over the posted limit (by any amount), the share FARS flags as speeding-related.
    # percent
    flagged_share_of_over_limit is count() { where: travel_speed_mph > posted_speed_limit_mph and speeding_related } / nullif(count() { where: travel_speed_mph > posted_speed_limit_mph }, 0)
    #" Occupants of these vehicles who died (excludes pedestrians and cyclists).
    occupant_deaths_total is occupant_deaths.sum()

  #" F4. By year, North Carolina vs rest of US: share of vehicles recorded 10+ mph over the limit (and any amount over) that FARS flags as speeding-related, like for like. NC records travel speed for ~95% of vehicles; the rest of the US for ~35-38%.
  view: nc_vs_rest_speeding_flag_on_fast_vehicles is {
    group_by: crashes.`year`, crashes.nc_or_rest_of_us
    aggregate: vehicles_10_over, flagged_share_of_10_over, flagged_share_of_over_limit
    order_by: `year`, nc_or_rest_of_us
  }

  #" By year: vehicles recorded 10+ mph over the limit and the share flagged speeding-related. Add `where: crashes.state_name = 'X'` for one state.
  view: speeding_flag_on_fast_vehicles is {
    group_by: crashes.`year`
    aggregate: vehicles_10_over, flagged_share_of_10_over
    order_by: `year`
  }
}

#" One row per person in a fatal crash (occupants, pedestrians, cyclists): role, age, sex, injury, belt or helmet use, location of nonoccupants, alcohol test and NHTSA's imputed BAC.
#(agent) `deaths` counts people killed; `people_involved` counts everyone (2.25x more). Motorcyclist is a vehicle type, not a person type: use `road_user`. Pedalcyclist counts before and after 2022 are not comparable (motorized bicycles moved from motorcyclists to pedalcyclists).
#(agent) `restraint` exists only for passenger-vehicle occupants. In the raw file the code for 'none used / not applicable' also covers motorcyclists; counting it as unbelted gives 74% too many in 2024. `unbelted_share` divides by known use (11% unknown), as NHTSA does.
#(agent) Dark = all three dark codes (lighted, not lighted, unknown lighting). `nonoccupant_location` is grouped from codes whose labels changed across years.
source: persons is motherduck.table('fars.persons') extend {
  primary_key: person_key
  join_one: crashes on crash_key = crashes.crash_key

  dimension:
    #" True when the crash happened in the dark (lighted, not lighted or unknown lighting).
    in_dark is crashes.light_condition ~ 'Dark%'

  measure:
    #" People killed.
    deaths is count() { where: is_killed }
    #" Everyone involved (killed, injured and uninjured). Not a death count.
    people_involved is count()
    #" People killed, valued at US DOT's VSL of $14.2 million each (2025 dollars); sums over the years in the query.
    # currency
    deaths_valued_at_vsl is count() { where: is_killed } * 14200000
    #" Passenger-vehicle occupants killed who were unrestrained.
    unbelted_deaths is count() { where: is_killed and restraint = 'Unrestrained' }
    #" Passenger-vehicle occupants killed whose restraint use is known.
    belt_known_deaths is count() { where: is_killed and restraint = ('Restrained' | 'Unrestrained') }
    #" Share of passenger-vehicle occupants killed who were unrestrained, of those with known use (NHTSA basis).
    # percent
    unbelted_share is count() { where: is_killed and restraint = 'Unrestrained' } / nullif(count() { where: is_killed and restraint = ('Restrained' | 'Unrestrained') }, 0)
    #" Pedestrians killed in the dark on non-freeway arterials.
    pedestrian_deaths_dark_arterial is count() { where: is_killed and road_user = 'Pedestrian' and in_dark and crashes.road_class = 'Arterial (not freeway)' }
    #" Pedestrians killed.
    pedestrian_deaths is count() { where: is_killed and road_user = 'Pedestrian' }
    #" Share of killed drivers with a known (tested and reported) BAC result.
    # percent
    killed_driver_bac_known_share is count() { where: is_killed and person_type = 'Driver' and bac_test_status = 'Tested, result known' } / nullif(count() { where: is_killed and person_type = 'Driver' }, 0)
    #" Of killed drivers with a known BAC result, the share at .08 or higher.
    # percent
    killed_driver_tested_positive_share is count() { where: is_killed and person_type = 'Driver' and tested_bac >= 0.08 } / nullif(count() { where: is_killed and person_type = 'Driver' and bac_test_status = 'Tested, result known' }, 0)
    #" People killed whose imputed BAC was .08+ (NHTSA imputation; drivers and nonoccupants only).
    deaths_own_bac_08_imputed is imputed_bac08_share.sum() { where: is_killed }

  #" F3. Pedestrian deaths by year: in the dark, on non-freeway arterials, at intersections. Add `where: crashes.county_fips = '37183'` for Wake County.
  view: pedestrian_deaths_dark_arterial_by_year is {
    where: road_user = 'Pedestrian'
    group_by: crashes.`year`
    aggregate:
      pedestrian_deaths
      dark_deaths is deaths { where: in_dark }
      pedestrian_deaths_dark_arterial
      # percent
      dark_arterial_share is pedestrian_deaths_dark_arterial / nullif(pedestrian_deaths, 0)
      at_intersection_deaths is deaths { where: nonoccupant_location = 'At intersection' }
      # percent
      at_intersection_share is deaths { where: nonoccupant_location = 'At intersection' } / nullif(pedestrian_deaths, 0)
      # currency
      dark_arterial_valued_at_vsl is pedestrian_deaths_dark_arterial * 14200000
    order_by: `year`
  }

  #" F2. Killed drivers by year, North Carolina vs rest of US: share with a known BAC result and, of those, share at .08+. In NC coverage in 2023-24 matched 2019-20 but the positive share fell (why is not known).
  view: nc_vs_rest_bac_known_killed_drivers is {
    group_by: crashes.`year`, crashes.nc_or_rest_of_us
    aggregate: killed_drivers is deaths { where: person_type = 'Driver' }, killed_driver_bac_known_share, killed_driver_tested_positive_share
    order_by: `year`, nc_or_rest_of_us
  }

  #" F3. Wake County vs rest of NC, all years in the query (2019-2024 unless filtered): pedestrians as a share of deaths, and the share of pedestrian deaths in the dark on non-freeway arterials or at intersections.
  view: wake_vs_rest_of_nc_pedestrians is {
    where: crashes.state_name = 'North Carolina'
    group_by: crashes.wake_or_rest_of_nc
    aggregate:
      deaths, pedestrian_deaths
      # percent
      pedestrian_share_of_deaths is pedestrian_deaths / nullif(deaths, 0)
      pedestrian_deaths_dark_arterial
      # percent
      dark_arterial_share_of_pedestrian_deaths is pedestrian_deaths_dark_arterial / nullif(pedestrian_deaths, 0)
      # percent
      at_intersection_share_of_pedestrian_deaths is deaths { where: road_user = 'Pedestrian' and nonoccupant_location = 'At intersection' } / nullif(pedestrian_deaths, 0)
  }

  #" Unrestrained share of passenger-vehicle occupants killed, by year and day vs night (known use only). Add `where: crashes.state_name = 'North Carolina'`; nationally the split matches NHTSA's published 56% night / 41% day (2024).
  view: unbelted_by_day_night is {
    where: road_user = 'Passenger vehicle occupant'
    group_by: crashes.`year`, crashes.day_night
    aggregate: deaths, belt_known_deaths, unbelted_deaths, unbelted_share
  }

  #" Context (published by NHTSA). Deaths by road user, 2019 vs 2024: occupant deaths are back near 2019 while motorcyclist and pedestrian deaths stay higher. Pedalcyclists cross the 2022 definition change.
  view: deaths_by_road_user_since_2019 is {
    group_by: road_user
    aggregate:
      deaths_2019 is deaths { where: `year` = 2019 }
      deaths_2024 is deaths { where: `year` = 2024 }
      change_since_2019 is deaths { where: `year` = 2024 } - deaths { where: `year` = 2019 }
      # percent
      pct_change_since_2019 is deaths { where: `year` = 2024 } / nullif(deaths { where: `year` = 2019 }, 0) - 1
      # currency
      change_valued_at_vsl is (deaths { where: `year` = 2024 } - deaths { where: `year` = 2019 }) * 14200000
    order_by: change_since_2019 desc
  }
}

#" Deaths and vehicle-miles (FHWA VM-2, millions) by year x state x rural/urban x road function; national figures are sums over states.
#(agent) Use for deaths per 100 million vehicle-miles by road type. Unfiltered queries pool every state and every year: filter `year` (and `state_name`) for a single-year or state rate. No county or city miles exist here: never divide county deaths by state miles. Rates include pedestrians, who do not drive the miles; they describe risk per mile, not the effect of any treatment. National figures are the sum over states (there is no 'United States' row here): leave `state_name` unfiltered for the US, filter it for one state, and filter `year` for one year. Deaths with unknown road type or rural/urban (490 in 2024) are not in this table.
#(agent) A few state cells report deaths but no miles (FHWA shows '-'): 2-4 deaths a year since 2022, 115 in 2021. Rates are per mile driven, not per mile of road. FHWA 2024 mileage is FHWA's October 2025 table. State sums of miles differ from FHWA's US total row by under 0.001%.
source: road_exposure is motherduck.table('fars.road_exposure') extend {
  where: state_name != 'United States'

  measure:
    #" People killed (crashes with known road type and rural/urban only).
    deaths_with_road_type is deaths.sum()
    #" Vehicle-miles travelled, millions.
    vmt_millions_total is vmt_millions.sum()
    #" Deaths per 100 million vehicle-miles.
    deaths_per_100m_vmt is deaths.sum() / nullif(vmt_millions.sum() / 100, 0)
    #" Deaths valued at US DOT's VSL ($14.2M, 2025 dollars); sums over the years in the query.
    # currency
    deaths_valued_at_vsl is deaths.sum() * 14200000

  #" Deaths per 100 million vehicle-miles by road class, with shares of deaths and miles and the rate relative to interstates and freeways. Refine with `+ { where: `year` = 2024 and state_name = 'North Carolina' }`; unfiltered it pools all states and years.
  view: deaths_per_mile_by_road_class is {
    group_by: road_class
    aggregate:
      deaths_with_road_type, vmt_millions_total, deaths_per_100m_vmt
      # percent
      share_of_deaths is deaths.sum() / all(deaths.sum())
      # percent
      share_of_miles is vmt_millions.sum() / all(vmt_millions.sum())
      rate_vs_interstate_freeway is (deaths.sum() / vmt_millions.sum()) / (all(deaths.sum() { where: road_class = 'Interstate or freeway' }) / all(vmt_millions.sum() { where: road_class = 'Interstate or freeway' }))
      deaths_valued_at_vsl
    order_by: deaths_per_100m_vmt desc
  }

  #" US 2024 (not final): deaths per 100 million vehicle-miles by road class.
  view: us_2024_deaths_per_mile_by_road_class is deaths_per_mile_by_road_class + { where: `year` = 2024 }

  #" F1. North Carolina vs US and rest of US deaths per 100 million vehicle-miles by road class, 2024 (not final) and pooled 2022-2024, with ratios to interstates and freeways. NC local-road miles jump 25% in 2024, so NC's 2024 local rate is unstable.
  view: nc_vs_us_deaths_per_mile_by_road_class is {
    group_by: road_class
    aggregate:
      nc_deaths_2024 is deaths.sum() { where: state_name = 'North Carolina' and `year` = 2024 }
      nc_rate_2024 is deaths.sum() { where: state_name = 'North Carolina' and `year` = 2024 } / (vmt_millions.sum() { where: state_name = 'North Carolina' and `year` = 2024 } / 100)
      nc_rate_2022_2024 is deaths.sum() { where: state_name = 'North Carolina' and `year` >= 2022 } / (vmt_millions.sum() { where: state_name = 'North Carolina' and `year` >= 2022 } / 100)
      us_rate_2024 is deaths.sum() { where: `year` = 2024 } / (vmt_millions.sum() { where: `year` = 2024 } / 100)
      us_rate_2022_2024 is deaths.sum() { where: `year` >= 2022 } / (vmt_millions.sum() { where: `year` >= 2022 } / 100)
      rest_of_us_rate_2022_2024 is deaths.sum() { where: state_name != 'North Carolina' and `year` >= 2022 } / (vmt_millions.sum() { where: state_name != 'North Carolina' and `year` >= 2022 } / 100)
      rest_of_us_ratio_vs_freeway_2022_2024 is (deaths.sum() { where: state_name != 'North Carolina' and `year` >= 2022 } / vmt_millions.sum() { where: state_name != 'North Carolina' and `year` >= 2022 }) / (all(deaths.sum() { where: state_name != 'North Carolina' and `year` >= 2022 and road_class = 'Interstate or freeway' }) / all(vmt_millions.sum() { where: state_name != 'North Carolina' and `year` >= 2022 and road_class = 'Interstate or freeway' }))
      nc_ratio_vs_freeway_2024 is (deaths.sum() { where: state_name = 'North Carolina' and `year` = 2024 } / vmt_millions.sum() { where: state_name = 'North Carolina' and `year` = 2024 }) / (all(deaths.sum() { where: state_name = 'North Carolina' and `year` = 2024 and road_class = 'Interstate or freeway' }) / all(vmt_millions.sum() { where: state_name = 'North Carolina' and `year` = 2024 and road_class = 'Interstate or freeway' }))
      nc_ratio_vs_freeway_2022_2024 is (deaths.sum() { where: state_name = 'North Carolina' and `year` >= 2022 } / vmt_millions.sum() { where: state_name = 'North Carolina' and `year` >= 2022 }) / (all(deaths.sum() { where: state_name = 'North Carolina' and `year` >= 2022 and road_class = 'Interstate or freeway' }) / all(vmt_millions.sum() { where: state_name = 'North Carolina' and `year` >= 2022 and road_class = 'Interstate or freeway' }))
      # currency
      nc_deaths_2024_valued_at_vsl is deaths.sum() { where: state_name = 'North Carolina' and `year` = 2024 } * 14200000
    order_by: nc_rate_2024 desc
  }

  #" F1 detail. North Carolina deaths per 100 million vehicle-miles by road function (major and minor collectors separate), 2024 and pooled 2022-2024. Minor-collector and local miles are state estimates.
  view: nc_deaths_per_mile_by_road_function is {
    where: state_name = 'North Carolina'
    group_by: road_function
    aggregate:
      deaths_2024 is deaths.sum() { where: `year` = 2024 }
      rate_2024 is deaths.sum() { where: `year` = 2024 } / (vmt_millions.sum() { where: `year` = 2024 } / 100)
      deaths_2022_2024 is deaths.sum() { where: `year` >= 2022 }
      rate_2022_2024 is deaths.sum() { where: `year` >= 2022 } / (vmt_millions.sum() { where: `year` >= 2022 } / 100)
    order_by: road_function
  }
}

#" One row per state (and 'United States') per year: deaths, alcohol-impaired-driving deaths three ways, vehicle-miles and population.
#(agent) Filter `state_name` to one value or exclude 'United States', or national totals are counted twice. The federal impaired-driving tier (23 CFR 1300.23) uses three final years: 2021-2023 until the 2024 Final File exists.
source: state_years is motherduck.table('fars.state_years') extend {
  measure:
    #" People killed.
    total_deaths is deaths.sum()
    #" Deaths per 100 million vehicle-miles.
    deaths_per_100m_vmt is deaths.sum() / nullif(vmt_millions.sum() / 100, 0)
    #" Deaths per 100,000 residents.
    deaths_per_100k_residents is deaths.sum() / nullif(population.sum() / 100000, 0)
    #" Alcohol-impaired-driving deaths per 100 million vehicle-miles (NHTSA imputed method): the rate in 23 CFR 1300.23.
    impaired_rate_per_100m_vmt is alcohol_impaired_deaths.sum() / nullif(vmt_millions.sum() / 100, 0)
    #" For comparison only: the same rate from tested BAC results only (undercounts).
    impaired_rate_tested_only is alcohol_deaths_tested_only.sum() / nullif(vmt_millions.sum() / 100, 0)

  #" F2. Federal impaired-driving tier per 23 CFR 1300.23 on 2021-2023 (low 0.30 or less, high 0.60 or more), computed with NHTSA's imputed counts and, for contrast, with tested results only. Our computation of the rule; NC's own FY2026 grant application states NC qualifies as mid-range, matching it.
  view: impaired_driving_tier_by_state is {
    where: `year` >= 2021 and `year` <= 2023 and state_name != 'United States'
    group_by: state_name
    aggregate: impaired_rate_per_100m_vmt, impaired_rate_tested_only
  } -> {
    select:
      state_name, impaired_rate_per_100m_vmt, impaired_rate_tested_only
      tier is impaired_rate_per_100m_vmt ? pick 'High' when >= 0.60 pick 'Mid' when > 0.30 else 'Low'
      tier_if_tested_only is impaired_rate_tested_only ? pick 'High' when >= 0.60 pick 'Mid' when > 0.30 else 'Low'
    order_by: impaired_rate_per_100m_vmt desc
  }

  #" Deaths per resident and per mile by year; add `where: state_name = 'North Carolina'`.
  view: rates_by_year is {
    group_by: `year`
    aggregate: total_deaths, deaths_per_100m_vmt, deaths_per_100k_residents
    order_by: `year`
  }
}

#" NHTSA's 10 imputed blood-alcohol values per crash: highest driver BAC and highest BAC among drivers and nonoccupants (g/dL).
#(agent) Do not average BACs; NHTSA's count averages the 10 results of the .08 test (that is `crashes.alcohol_impaired_deaths`). Use this source to see the imputation spread.
source: alcohol_imputations is motherduck.table('fars.alcohol_imputations') extend {
  join_one: crashes on crash_key = crashes.crash_key
  measure:
    #" Deaths in crashes whose highest driver BAC is .08+ in this imputation.
    impaired_deaths_in_imputation is crashes.fatalities.sum() { where: max_driver_bac >= 0.08 }

  #" Alcohol-impaired-driving deaths under each of the 10 imputations, by year (the spread is the imputation uncertainty).
  view: imputation_spread is {
    group_by: `year`, imputation
    aggregate: impaired_deaths_in_imputation
    order_by: `year`, imputation
  }
}

#" US Census county population estimates, 2019-2024 (Vintage 2020 for 2019, Vintage 2025 for 2020-2024).
source: county_population is motherduck.table('fars.county_population') extend {
  measure:
    #" Residents.
    residents is population.sum()
}

#" North Carolina speeding-related deaths by year under two official definitions: FARS (NHTSA) and NCDOT's published state crash data, beside deaths in crashes with a vehicle recorded 10+ mph over the limit.
#(agent) FARS's NC count has swung before (566 in 2016, 327 in 2018 per NC's highway safety plan chart). NC's plan's 2019-2023 average (412.6) splices FARS 2019-2021 (2021 is the ARF value 478; Final File 487) with NCDOT 2022 (426) and an extrapolated 2023 (340, from the first three months).
#(agent) NC's highway safety plan names FARS as the source of its speeding target (C-6) but sets the baseline from NCDOT data. FARS rose 517 -> 676 (2022-2024) while NCDOT fell 426 -> 378 and deaths with a vehicle 10+ mph over the limit fell 634 -> 598: the FARS rise appears to be in how crashes are flagged (inferred; who changed what is not documented), not in recorded speeds.
source: nc_speeding_definitions is motherduck.table('fars.nc_speeding_definitions') extend {
  #" F4. NC speeding-related deaths by year: FARS flag vs NCDOT's published count vs crashes with a vehicle 10+ mph over the limit.
  view: nc_speeding_two_definitions is {
    select: `year`, deaths, fars_speeding_related_deaths, ncdot_speed_related_deaths, deaths_vehicle_10_over_limit
      fars_over_ncdot is fars_speeding_related_deaths / ncdot_speed_related_deaths
    order_by: `year`
  }
}
